{{/*
  The hand-over to the gateway, in the two shapes a gateway comes in.

  A form post (PayFast) is a real cross-origin submission, not a redirect, because
  the gateway wants the fields posted to it — which is why the CSP has to name the
  gateway's origin in form-action, and why the vendored submit-on-load script
  exists: with script-src 'self' and no inline script, an onload attribute would
  simply be blocked. The button is not a fallback nobody sees: without JavaScript
  it is the whole mechanism, and it is deliberately labelled so that pressing it is
  obviously the right thing to do.

  A link (SnapScan) is not submitted for the shopper at all, and must not be: the
  page is the instructions, and a page that navigates away before it is read is no
  instructions. It shows both ways to pay, because one page serves two devices —
  the QR code for a desktop, where the phone doing the paying is not the thing
  reading the screen, and the button for a phone, where the same URL opens the
  SnapScan app directly. Which is emphasised is decided by CSS at a width, not by
  sniffing a user agent here.
*/}}
{{define "content"}}

{{if .Handover.IsLink}}

<h1>Pay with {{.GatewayLabel}}</h1>
<p>Order <strong>{{.Order.Reference}}</strong> — {{.Currency}} {{money .Order.TotalCents}}</p>

<div class="qr-handover">
  <a class="button qr-handover-button" href="{{.Handover.Action}}">Open {{.GatewayLabel}} to pay</a>

  <figure class="qr-handover-code">
    {{/*
      No width or height attributes: SnapScan's documentation is explicit that
      setting them stretches the code and makes it unscannable. The size comes
      from the stylesheet, and from the snap_code_size the image URL asks for.
    */}}
    <img src="{{.Handover.QRImageURL}}" alt="{{.GatewayLabel}} payment code for order {{.Order.Reference}}">
    <figcaption>Scan this with the {{.GatewayLabel}} app on your phone.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

{{/*
  Nothing here redirects, so the page asks instead: it polls for the order's
  status and says so when the money arrives. Without JavaScript it shows the
  waiting message and the confirmation still arrives by email, which is the
  ordinary failure mode and an acceptable one.
*/}}
<div id="payment-status" hx-get="/cart/checkout/status" hx-trigger="load delay:5s" hx-swap="outerHTML">
  <p class="muted">Your confirmation is emailed as soon as the payment goes through.</p>
</div>

<p><a href="/cart">← Back to your cart</a></p>

{{else}}

<h1>Sending you to pay</h1>
<p>Order <strong>{{.Order.Reference}}</strong> — {{.Currency}} {{money .Order.TotalCents}}</p>
<p>You are being handed over to {{.GatewayLabel}} to pay. If nothing happens, press the button.</p>

<form id="gateway-redirect" method="post" action="{{.Handover.Action}}">
  {{range .Handover.Fields}}<input type="hidden" name="{{.Name}}" value="{{.Value}}">
  {{end}}
  <button type="submit">Continue to {{.GatewayLabel}}</button>
</form>
<script src="{{asset "redirect.js"}}" defer></script>

{{end}}

{{end}}
